<body>
<main>

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">Lesson 1 &middot; 30 minutes</div>
    <h1>Choose the right knife and cut for a job</h1>
    <p class="lead">By the end of this lesson you can look at a job in your kitchen, such as slicing a tomato for a sandwich or dicing an onion for soup, and name the knife and the cut that suit it.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Objective O2: choose the knife (chef's, paring, serrated) and the cut (slice, dice, julienne)</span>
      <span>About 30 minutes</span>
    </div>
    <div class="box mission">
      <span class="label">Why this matters for you</span>
      <p>You want to cut properly and safely. A knife that is wrong for the job makes you push, saw and slip, and slipping is how fingers get cut. A cut that is wrong for the dish cooks unevenly. Picking well is a safety skill as much as a cooking skill.</p>
      <p>You already hold the knife and the food the safe way (pinch grip and claw grip). Now you decide <em>what</em> to hold.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="In this lesson">
    <strong>In this lesson</strong>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#warm-up">Warm-up</a></li>
      <li><a href="#knives">Three knives, three jobs</a></li>
      <li><a href="#cuts">Slice, dice and julienne</a></li>
      <li><a href="#match">Matching the job to the knife and the cut</a></li>
      <li><a href="#mixups">Common mix-ups</a></li>
      <li><a href="#practice">Worked example and practice</a></li>
      <li><a href="#summary">Summary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#further">Go further</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section id="warm-up">
    <h2>Warm-up <span class="section-time">2 min</span></h2>
    <p>Last time you learned how to hold the knife and the food. Before we add anything new, pull that back from memory.</p>
    <div class="check">
      <span class="label">Recall</span>
      <p>Without looking back: what does your food hand do in the claw grip, and what part of that hand does the side of the blade touch?</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Check your answer</summary>
        <p>The fingertips curl back, away from the blade, and the knuckles guide the flat side of the blade. This lesson builds on it: every cut below is done with that same grip. Choosing the right knife and cut changes <em>what</em> you cut, never how your hands stay safe.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="knives">
    <h2>Three knives, three jobs <span class="section-time">7 min</span></h2>
    <p>Start with a kitchen you know. You have a drawer with a big knife, a small knife, and maybe one with a toothed edge for bread. Those three are enough for almost everything a home cook does, and each has a different shape for a reason.</p>
    <p>A <dfn>chef's knife</dfn> is the big, all-purpose knife with a broad blade that curves up toward the tip. Cooking schools describe it as the workhorse of the kitchen, used for chopping, slicing, dicing and mincing (<a href="https://escoffier.edu/blog/culinary-arts/different-knives-and-the-best-uses-for-each">Escoffier</a>). A typical blade is about 8 to 10 inches long. The long, broad blade gives you a flat side to guide with your knuckles, and enough weight to cut through firm vegetables such as carrots, onions and potatoes without much force.</p>
    <p>A <dfn>paring knife</dfn> is the small knife, with a blade of roughly 2 to 4 inches. It is for work in your hand or close to the board that a big blade is clumsy at: peeling, trimming, coring a tomato, hulling strawberries, taking the stem out of a pepper (<a href="https://escoffier.edu/blog/culinary-arts/different-knives-and-the-best-uses-for-each">Escoffier</a>).</p>
    <p>A <dfn>serrated knife</dfn> has a toothed edge, like a tiny saw. Its job is food with a tough or slippery outside and a soft inside: bread, tomatoes, and also citrus and cake. The teeth bite through the skin, so you do not have to press down hard on the soft middle and squash it. Serrated knives are known above all as bread knives, and they also handle thin but resistant skins like a tomato's (<a href="https://escoffier.edu/blog/culinary-arts/different-knives-and-the-best-uses-for-each">Escoffier</a>).</p>

    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig1-title">
        <title id="fig1-title">Three knife blades drawn side by side: a long, broad chef's knife, a short paring knife and a long serrated knife with a toothed edge.</title>
        <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="18" fill="currentColor">
          <!-- chef's knife -->
          <text x="20" y="30" font-weight="600">Chef's knife</text>
          <path d="M20,60 L360,60 Q380,60 400,90 L400,92 L20,92 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="-90" y="60" width="0" height="0"/>
          <rect x="400" y="64" width="110" height="24" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="520" y="82" style="fill: var(--muted)">8–10 in</text>
          <!-- paring knife -->
          <text x="20" y="140" font-weight="600">Paring knife</text>
          <path d="M20,170 L130,170 Q150,172 160,190 L20,190 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="160" y="172" width="80" height="18" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="260" y="188" style="fill: var(--muted)">2–4 in</text>
          <!-- serrated -->
          <text x="20" y="240" font-weight="600">Serrated knife</text>
          <path d="M20,260 L400,260 L400,290 L20,290 Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <path d="M20,290 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12 l10,-12 l10,12" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="400" y="264" width="110" height="22" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="520" y="282" style="fill: var(--muted)">teeth</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>Figure 1. Compare the blades, not the handles. The chef's knife is long and broad, the paring knife is short and fine, and the serrated knife has teeth along its edge (drawn in colour). Sizes are typical, not exact; your own drawer may differ.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <aside class="note">You do not need more than these three to start. Cooking-school lists add many more (boning, slicing, cleaver and so on), but for a home cook these three cover most jobs.</aside>

    <h3>How the shape decides the job</h3>
    <p>Ask two questions about the food. First, <strong>how big is it, and how firm?</strong> Big and firm (a butternut squash, an onion, a carrot) calls for the chef's knife, since its length and weight do the work. Small or fiddly (a strawberry's stem, a pepper's core) calls for the paring knife. Second, <strong>is the outside tougher than the inside?</strong> If so (bread, tomato), reach for the serrated knife.</p>

    <div class="check" data-check>
      <span class="label">Check yourself</span>
      <p>You want to cut a ripe tomato into thin slices for a sandwich. The skin is slippery and the inside is soft. Which knife do you take?</p>
      <ul class="choices">
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="false" data-why="The paring knife is too short for a clean slice across a whole tomato, so you end up sawing and pressing.">The paring knife, since a small blade is more careful</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="false" data-why="A chef's knife can slice tomatoes if it is very sharp, but a blunt one will skid on the skin and squash the middle. It is not the best match here.">The chef's knife, since it is the all-purpose workhorse</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="true" data-why="Tough skin over a soft inside is exactly what the toothed edge is for: the teeth bite the skin so you do not have to press down.">The serrated knife, since its teeth bite the tough skin</button></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <details>
        <summary>Show the answer</summary>
        <p>The serrated knife. The teeth grip and break the skin, then the blade glides through the soft middle without squashing it.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="cuts">
    <h2>Slice, dice and julienne <span class="section-time">7 min</span></h2>
    <p>The knife is how you cut. The <dfn>cut</dfn> is the shape you cut the food into. Recipes name cuts so that the pieces are the same size, because pieces of the same size cook in the same time. If a recipe says "dice the onion" and you chop it into random chunks, some bits will burn while others stay raw.</p>
    <p>Here are the three cuts in this lesson. Sizes come from cooking-school standards, and different books vary a little, so treat them as targets, not laws (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_culinary_knife_cuts">Wikipedia's list of culinary knife cuts</a>; <a href="https://rouxbe.com/tips-techniques/495">Rouxbe</a>).</p>

    <h3>Slice</h3>
    <p>A <dfn>slice</dfn> is a flat piece cut across the food: bread slices, tomato slices, rounds of cucumber. It is the simplest cut, and the starting point for the other two. The one thing to choose is thickness: thin for a sandwich, thicker for roasting.</p>

    <h3>Julienne</h3>
    <p><dfn>Julienne</dfn> (say "ZHOO-lee-en") means thin, matchstick-shaped strips. The usual school size is about 1/8 inch (3 mm) thick and 1/8 inch wide, and around 2 to 2½ inches (5 to 6 cm) long (<a href="https://escoffier.edu/blog/culinary-arts/different-knives-and-the-best-uses-for-each">Escoffier</a>, as summarised by search; <a href="https://rouxbe.com/tips-techniques/495">Rouxbe</a>). Think of the carrot sticks in a stir-fry or a slaw. They are thin so they cook fast or can be eaten raw.</p>
    <p>A slightly thicker stick, about 1/4 inch square, is called a <dfn>batonnet</dfn> ("bah-toh-NAY"). Think of a French fry. If you cut julienne you are cutting a thinner version of the same idea.</p>

    <h3>Dice</h3>
    <p>To <dfn>dice</dfn> is to cut food into small cubes. Dice comes in sizes. A <em>small dice</em> is 1/4 inch (about 6 mm) on every side. A <em>medium dice</em> is about 1/2 inch (12 mm), and a <em>large dice</em> is about 3/4 inch (about 2 cm) (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_culinary_knife_cuts">Wikipedia</a>; <a href="https://rouxbe.com/tips-techniques/495">Rouxbe</a>). Even smaller cubes, 1/8 inch, are called <dfn>brunoise</dfn>, but you can leave that for later.</p>

    <figure>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig2-title">
        <title id="fig2-title">One carrot becomes slices, then sticks, then cubes. A flat slice, a thin julienne stick, a thicker batonnet stick and a small cube, drawn with their sizes.</title>
        <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="18" fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle">
          <!-- slice -->
          <ellipse cx="80" cy="90" rx="50" ry="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <ellipse cx="80" cy="100" rx="50" ry="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="80" y="170" font-weight="600">Slice</text>
          <text x="80" y="195" style="fill: var(--muted)">flat piece</text>
          <!-- julienne -->
          <rect x="200" y="50" width="8" height="100" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="216" y="50" width="8" height="100" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="232" y="50" width="8" height="100" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="220" y="170" font-weight="600">Julienne</text>
          <text x="220" y="195" style="fill: var(--muted)">1/8 in thin sticks</text>
          <!-- batonnet -->
          <rect x="330" y="50" width="20" height="100" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="360" y="50" width="20" height="100" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="355" y="170" font-weight="600">Batonnet</text>
          <text x="355" y="195" style="fill: var(--muted)">1/4 in sticks</text>
          <!-- dice -->
          <rect x="480" y="100" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <rect x="530" y="100" width="40" height="40" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
          <text x="525" y="170" font-weight="600">Small dice</text>
          <text x="525" y="195" style="fill: var(--muted)">1/4 in cubes</text>
          <!-- arrows -->
          <text x="320" y="260" style="fill: var(--muted)" font-size="16">Sticks cut across become cubes: batonnet first, then dice.</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>Figure 2. The cuts are one family. Slices stacked and cut lengthwise become sticks; sticks bundled and cut across become cubes. The shapes are not to scale.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <aside class="note">Why the matching sizes matter: pieces of different sizes cook at different speeds. Matching sizes is the whole point of naming cuts.</aside>

    <div class="check" data-check>
      <span class="label">Check yourself</span>
      <p>A soup recipe says "small dice the carrots." What shape and size are you aiming for?</p>
      <ul class="choices">
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="true" data-why="Dice means cubes, and small dice is about 1/4 inch on every side.">Cubes of about 1/4 inch on each side</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="false" data-why="That describes julienne, which is thin sticks and not cubes. The two cuts are related but not the same.">Thin matchsticks of about 1/8 inch wide</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="false" data-why="Rounds are slices. Dice needs cubes, so you cut again in the other directions.">Flat rounds of about 1/4 inch thick</button></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <details>
        <summary>Show the answer</summary>
        <p>Cubes of about 1/4 inch each side. Julienne is matchsticks and a slice is a flat round.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="match">
    <h2>Matching the job to the knife and the cut <span class="section-time">5 min</span></h2>
    <p>Now put the two halves together. For any job, answer three questions in this order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>What shape does the dish want?</strong> Flat pieces, thin sticks, or cubes? (That picks the cut.)</li>
      <li><strong>How big and firm is the food?</strong> (That picks between chef's and paring.)</li>
      <li><strong>Is the outside tougher than the inside?</strong> (That points to serrated.)</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Here are everyday jobs, answered:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Knife</th><th>Cut</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Onion for soup</td><td>Chef's</td><td>Dice</td><td>Firm and large; cubes cook evenly in a pot.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carrot sticks for a stir-fry</td><td>Chef's</td><td>Julienne</td><td>Thin sticks cook in a minute or two.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tomato for a sandwich</td><td>Serrated</td><td>Slice</td><td>Slippery skin, soft inside.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Loaf of bread</td><td>Serrated</td><td>Slice</td><td>Hard crust, soft crumb.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Core a pepper or hull strawberries</td><td>Paring</td><td>None (trimming)</td><td>Small, close work in your hand.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Potato for roasting</td><td>Chef's</td><td>Large dice</td><td>Big cubes brown outside while staying soft inside.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Notice that a paring knife job sometimes has no cut name at all. Peeling and trimming are about preparing the food, not shaping it.</p>

    <div class="check" data-check>
      <span class="label">Check yourself</span>
      <p>You are making a quick slaw and want long, thin strips of a firm, large cabbage-stem and a carrot. Which pair fits best?</p>
      <ul class="choices">
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="false" data-why="Serrated knives suit tough skin over soft insides. A carrot is firm all the way through, and dice makes cubes, not strips.">Serrated knife, to cut small dice</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="false" data-why="Paring knives are for small close work. A long strip of a large carrot needs a long blade and a steady board.">Paring knife, to cut thin sticks</button></li>
        <li><button type="button" data-correct="true" data-why="Firm and large calls for the chef's knife, and thin strips are julienne.">Chef's knife, to cut julienne</button></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="feedback" aria-live="polite"></p>
      <details>
        <summary>Show the answer</summary>
        <p>Chef's knife, julienne: firm and large food, and thin sticks.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="mixups">
    <h2>Common mix-ups <span class="section-time">3 min</span></h2>
    <div class="box misconception">
      <span class="label">A common mix-up: "the small knife is the safe knife"</span>
      <p>It is tempting because a small blade looks less scary. But a paring knife is short, so you hold the food in your hand and cut toward it, and that is the situation where fingers get cut. For a firm vegetable on a board, the long chef's knife with your claw grip is the safer tool. Pick the knife for the job, not for how it looks.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="box misconception">
      <span class="label">A common mix-up: "the serrated knife cuts everything"</span>
      <p>The teeth look like they would help on anything. On a firm vegetable they tear instead of cutting cleanly, and the ragged cut makes uneven pieces. Keep the serrated knife for bread and tomatoes and other foods with a tough outside and a soft inside.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="box misconception">
      <span class="label">A common mix-up: "dice and chop are the same, so size does not matter"</span>
      <p>To chop is to cut roughly. To dice is to cut into cubes of a named size. If a recipe says "dice" it wants even cubes, because even pieces cook at the same speed. A rough chop is fine for a rustic stew, but not when the recipe names the cut.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="note">The dull knife idea from your notes belongs to the next lesson, on sharpening. For now, one thing: a sharp knife lets you cut without pushing hard, and pushing hard is when knives slip.</aside>
  </section>

  <section id="practice">
    <h2>Worked example and practice <span class="section-time">5 min</span></h2>
    <div class="box worked">
      <span class="label">Worked example</span>
      <p>Job: a stir-fry needs a carrot cut into julienne. Which knife, which cut, and how?</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pick the cut.</strong> A stir-fry cooks in minutes, so the pieces must be thin. Thin sticks are julienne.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick the knife.</strong> A carrot is firm and long, so the chef's knife.</li>
        <li><strong>Make a flat face.</strong> Trim the ends, then cut a thin slice off one side so the carrot lies flat and cannot roll. A rolling carrot is a slipping carrot.</li>
        <li><strong>Slice into planks.</strong> Lay it on the flat side and cut lengthwise slices about 1/8 inch thick, cutting about 2 to 2½ inches long. Keep the claw grip.</li>
        <li><strong>Cut the sticks.</strong> Stack a few planks, then cut them lengthwise into 1/8 inch strips. The result is julienne.</li>
        <li><strong>Check.</strong> The sticks should look like matchsticks and be about the same size. Uneven sticks will cook unevenly, so recut the odd ones.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="check">
      <span class="label">Your turn</span>
      <p>Job: a potato needs to be cut into small dice for a quick hash. Decide the knife, then write the steps. The first step is given: "Pick the knife: the potato is firm and large, so the chef's knife." Now work out the rest: how do you make it stable, and how do you get from slices to cubes?</p>
      <details>
        <summary>Show a solution</summary>
        <p>Make a flat face by slicing off one side so the potato does not roll. Lay it on the flat side and cut planks about 1/4 inch thick. Stack a few planks and cut them lengthwise into sticks 1/4 inch wide (batonnet). Gather the sticks and cut across them every 1/4 inch to make small dice of about 1/4 inch. Check that the cubes are about the same size.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>Summary <span class="section-time">1 min</span></h2>
    <div class="box summary">
      <ul>
        <li>Chef's knife for big, firm food; paring knife for small, close work; serrated knife for a tough outside and a soft inside.</li>
        <li>Slice is a flat piece, julienne is thin matchsticks (about 1/8 inch), and dice is cubes (small dice is about 1/4 inch).</li>
        <li>Decide the cut from the dish, then the knife from the food.</li>
        <li>Matching sizes means even cooking.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Say it in your own words:</strong> without looking up, explain to an imaginary friend which knife and cut you would use for an onion in soup, and why.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

